 @Tofuey  Like Linus said before, AMD is not your friend. They're a business that needs to make a profit to continue staying in business. Their CPUs and GPUs itself may not cost $200 to make, but they still have to pay for past and future R&D cost as well as whatever other expenses they have. 

I agree that in a "normal" situation, this card is overpriced at $200. But in a market where an RX 580 is selling for over $400, it's a fair deal. Not a great one, but better than the alternative. If more gamers can get entry level cards that provide a good enough experience without paying scalpers or waiting hours in line at Best Buy or Micro Center, I'm ok with it. 

At least they're making an effort compared to team green who's releasing a new high end GPU or a RTX 2060 for $600+ that no one was asking for. We still need to see how the 3050 launches, but if it turns out to just be a paper launch then the 6500 is really going to be the only option for "budget" gaming.

 @TheAnoniemo  "if you want to make a product as cheap as possible to produce, it makes sense that you will leave performance on the table"

That's not really how they're treating the 6500xt though. They are literally crippling it by saving maybe 20usd per card at most, a cost that 90% of anyone who even considers this card can reasonably affort to pay extra. It's just a poorly made card, for people who don't know what they are missing out on. It will go into low-end prebuilts and state "can run XYZ games on 60fps", but it won't state that for 20usd extra the performance could literally have been 50% greater. For these kind of sales it doesn't matter if you cripple your product by 50%. As long as that crippled product is still within the margin of playing certain game titles at bearable fps, then that's what the majority of consumers will pay attention to. Thus they are exploiting people who simply don't know any better.

It's like selling a house that is extremely underequipped at a high pricepoint for what you effectively get, but then it's still technically marketed as "cheapest in the area" and people who just can't afford what is more expensive, will look at this house and accept it because at least it offers a roof over their head. Even though that "roof over their head" technically should only cost half of what it does, but lack of price competition means it can be sold at any price as long as it is less than whatever the neighbor house is charging.

 @ThatLaloBoy  True but AMD has literally earned more in the past 2 years during shortage, than they have done in any prior time period. They don't need to squeeze out every last cent on the production of their budget products, especially considering they're already selling way above typical msrp. They just do it because they can, because extra money is good business. They could have used this situation to say:

"hey we have earned a lot of excess profit the past 2 years, maybe we don't treat our next product as a cashgrab and instead price it fairly (or make it actually decent) so that we will get good publicity and earn loyalty even with the customers of our competition".

But they chose the cashgrab method even though they didn't have to. It is very simple, and people are digging too deep into their reasoning behind it. There is a market, there is a need. And they're squeezing as much quick cash out of that need as they are able to. They didn't think of how this could work well for their brand image long term, it was a quick and easy job.

 @DimiS1978  The biggest hit is from the 4GB memory and limited bandwidth of the memory bus, as was pointed out.  If you play games with this, and this is a 1080p gaming GPU, it's not meant for the best quality settings for newer games.  You have to step it down just like you had to in the past with a RX 470 4GB or RX 550.    Or really, the hit going from PCIe gen4 to gen 3 X4, is basically the same as the hit in having a fast processor that's limited to 4GB in these different games.  I don't think the hits would be a summation, more like since you take a hit if you run PCIe gen3, you'd get about the same hit anyway because it's a 4GB GPU that suffers from a lower bandwidth.

And, unlike the review, this is equivalent to something that would have cost $140 USD back in 2017.  Inflation since then is more like 15% in the world of electronics, and THEN since these come from China, the 2 main markets for GPUs, the EU and US, or N. America have large import taxes added that the companies have to pay.  LTT talked about this back in Feb 2021 but for some reason can't remember this is still in effect.  In the US, it's a 25% tariff and it affects Canada too, and the EU countries add a 20 - 25% VAT.  Other countries also add import taxes, but these large taxes from the EU and US have been added 2020 and 2021, and for the US it started Jan 1, 2021.  The companies gave press releases saying their prices were going up because of it.

So this is what you get now for $200 USD.  Don't expect it to get any better until GPUs are made outside of China.  And, that doesn't do anything for the high inflation.  That only gets rid of the high import taxes.  There would have to be deflation in prices around the world to get back to prices a couple years ago.

